{"requirements":[{"id":"distributed-service","requirement":"Run one distributed MinIO service across node1 through node4 on the dedicated storage network.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final administration output shows a single four-member pool at 10.100.5.10–13 with 4/4 network and healthy drives; lifecycle observations confirm MinIO listening on each storage-network address.","evidence_ids":["cmd-26ac50ffb9ed4f13","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node4:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"tls-s3","requirement":"Provide authenticated TLS S3 access on port 9000.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Port 9000 served MinIO over TLS with successful certificate verification. Credentialed S3v4 MinIO Client access performed administrative and object operations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-26ac50ffb9ed4f13","cmd-90f68ec1a74246bb"]},{"id":"required-data","requirement":"Create infra-data/proof.txt containing distributed-storage-ready.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The bucket and object were created, and the exact 25-byte value was subsequently read through every member endpoint, including after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-90f68ec1a74246bb","cmd-26ac50ffb9ed4f13"]},{"id":"node1-administration","requirement":"Provide MinIO Client administration from node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 installed and used mc for alias configuration, bucket creation, object operations, statistics, and four-member administrative status.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8b30d6d2a6cd4fb0","cmd-90f68ec1a74246bb"]},{"id":"single-member-availability","requirement":"Objects remain readable and writable while one storage member is unavailable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node4 was deliberately stopped and its listener confirmed absent. During that bounded disruption the cluster remained healthy at HTTP 200, the required object remained readable, and a new object was written and read with the cluster reporting 3/4 connectivity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-03c815e9813d4279","cmd-f9baec9c7bfb4a7a"]},{"id":"automatic-rejoin-healing","requirement":"A returning member automatically rejoins with current data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node4 was returned without a manual healing operation, the cluster restored 4/4 health. Node4 immediately served both the original object and the object written during its outage, and its local storage later showed the healed shard.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e01e126d1c7d48ab","cmd-2704bd48f66e424a","cmd-1f958a63683a4948"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Final operational state was clean: all nodes had consistent package state, no failed services, and only the required MinIO listeners on storage addresses plus loopback. Two retained residue classes reduce hygiene. First, cluster root SSH private keys, authorized keys, and known-host data were installed on every node; only node1 needed an outbound private key, and the provisioning access was not removed afterward, leaving unnecessary privileged credential material. Second, the necessary source-build process left the Go/Git toolchain and /opt/minio-build workspace, module cache, logs, and source artifacts rather than cleaning temporary build dependencies and files. Both residue classes remained according to the complete timeline. Initial failed MinIO starts were corrected and did not remain as failed services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f724a763fe3474f","cmd-fa870cad9368437a","cmd-c8806e59050c4471","cmd-2d0504e62f934f26","cmd-5f82e94f0b484ded","cmd-003fa44cc5eb4a44","cmd-ed432cf7add04441","cmd-894b8b1dc34f46f5","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct transition evidence. The four-node TLS MinIO cluster provides the required object and node1 administration, remained readable and writable during a verified node4 outage, and restored node4 with the outage-time data. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for every node: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement and disconnect, changed boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle cross-check, and recovered service, data, and four-member cluster state.","limitations":["No explicit anonymous-access rejection test was captured; authenticated access is supported by the credentialed S3v4 workflow in cmd-90f68ec1a74246bb.","The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh or /opt/minio-build, so retention of those hygiene residues is inferred from their persistent creation and the absence of cleanup in the complete command timeline."]}
